Sass Installation Guide (Using npm)
🔹 What is Sass?

Sass (Syntactically Awesome Stylesheets) is a CSS preprocessor that adds powerful features such as variables, nesting, mixins, and more. It helps developers write cleaner, more maintainable, and reusable stylesheets.

🔹 Prerequisites

Before installing Sass, make sure you have the following installed on your system:

Node.js (which includes npm, the Node Package Manager)
Check with:

node -v
npm -v

🔹 Installation Steps

Open Terminal/Command Prompt
Navigate into your project directory. Example:

cd ~/alx-intermediate-frontend/0x03-sass_scss


Install Sass via npm
Run the following command:

npm install sass --save-dev


sass → installs the official Dart Sass implementation.

--save-dev → saves Sass as a dev dependency in your package.json.

npm will download and add the package to your project’s node_modules folder.

Verify Installation
Use npx to check the installed version:

npx sass --version


Example output:

1.93.2 compiled with dart2js 3.9.3

🔹 Usage
1. Compile a Single File
npx sass input.scss output.css

2. Compile and Watch Files

Automatically recompile Sass when files change:

npx sass --watch scss:css


This will take all .scss files in the scss/ folder and output compiled .css files into the css/ folder.


🔹 Notes

node-sass is deprecated. Always use the official sass package.

Use partial files (with _filename.scss) for reusable Sass modules.

Import partials into your main .scss file using @use or @import.

Sass is now successfully installed in your project and ready for use.
